#v(0.8em)

#smallcaps[In] the particular declaration of wives’ duties, the apostle notes two points:

+ The #emph[duty] required.
+ The #emph[reason] to enforce it.

In setting out the duty, he declares

+ The #emph[matter] in which it consists.
+ The #emph[manner] in which it is to be performed.

In the matter we may note:

+ The thing required, #footnote[ὑποτάσσεσθε.] #emph[subjection.]
+ The persons it concerns, their #footnote[ἰδίοις.] #emph[own] husbands.

The manner concerns the #emph[quality] and the #emph[extent] of that subjection.

To declare the quality of wives’ subjection to their husbands, two rules are set down.

1\. That it is to be such a subjection as #footnote[As to the Lord.] should be performed to Christ.

2\. That it is to be such a subjection as the #footnote[As the church is subject to Christ.] church performs to Christ.

The extent of wives’ subjection stretches very far, even to #emph[all things.]#footnote[ἐν παντί, In everything.]

The reason to enforce all these points is taken from that place of eminence and authority in which the husband is set above his wife. This reason is

1\. Set forth under the metaphor of a head (“for the husband is the head of the wife”).

2\. Amplified by the resemblance he has in this to Christ.

In this resemblance two points are noted.

1\. That the husband, by virtue of his place, carries the very #emph[image] of Christ (“as Christ is the head of the church”).

2\. That the husband, by virtue of his office, is a protector of his wife (“and He is the Savior of the body”).

=== §. 2. #emph[Of a wife’s subjection in general.]#footnote(numbering: (..) => "*")[Treatise 4, §. 2.]

The first point to be handled in the treatise of wives’ particular duties is the general matter of them all #emph[(subjection),] under which all the other particulars are included. For it has as large #footnote[#emph[Maritum habere Dominum meruit mulieris culpa, quod tamen nisi seruetur, deprauabitur ampliùs natura, & augebitur culpa. Aug. de Gen. ad lit. lib.] 11. #emph[c.] 37. \[Through the woman’s fault she deserved to have her husband as lord; yet unless this is kept, her nature will be further corrupted and her fault increased.\] #emph[Ante peccatum non aliter factam fuisse decet credere mulierem nisi vt vir ei dominaretur. Ibid.] \[Before the sin it is right to believe that the woman was made on no other terms than that the man should rule over her.\]] an extent as that #emph[honor] which is required in the fifth commandment, when it is applied to wives. When the Lord first declared her duty to the woman, he set it down in this phrase: “Your desire will be subject to your husband” (Gen 3:16).

#emph[Objection.] Was that not a punishment inflicted on her for her transgression?

#emph[Answer.] It was a law too, for the testing of her obedience; and if it is not kept, her nature will be more depraved and her fault more increased. Besides, we cannot help thinking that the woman was made before the fall so that the man might rule over her. On this ground the prophets and apostles have often urged the same. Sarah is commended for this, that she was #emph[subject] to her husband (1 Pet 3:6). By this the Holy Spirit would teach wives that #emph[subjection] ought to be like salt, to season every duty which they perform to their husbands. Their very opinion, feeling, speech, action, and all that concerns the husband, must taste of #emph[subjection.] Contrary to this is the disposition of many wives, whom ambition has tainted and corrupted within and without. They cannot bear to hear of #emph[subjection:] they imagine that it makes them slaves. But I hope that partly by what has been delivered before about those common duties which man and wife mutually owe each other, and partly by the particulars which are included under this general, but most of all #footnote[#emph[Quem vocavit ad culpam mulier, iustum est vt eum gubernatorem assumat, ne iterum foeminea facilitate labatur. Ambr. Hexaem. l.] 5. #emph[c.] 7.] by the duties which the husband in particular owes his wife, it will plainly appear that this #emph[subjection] is no servitude. But even if it were more than it is, seeing God requires subjection of a wife to her husband, the wife is bound to yield it. And it is good reason that she who first drew man into sin should now be subject to him, so that she does not fall again through the like womanish weakness.

=== §. 3. #emph[Of a husband’s superiority over a wife, to be acknowledged by a wife.]#footnote(numbering: (..) => "*")[Treatise 4, §. 6, 7.]

The subjection which is required of a wife to her husband implies two things.

+ That she acknowledges her husband to be her superior.
+ That she respects him as her superior.

That acknowledgment of the husband’s superiority is twofold:

+ General, of any husband.
+ Particular, of her own husband.

The general is the ground of the particular. For until a wife is taught that a husband, by virtue of his place, is his wife’s superior, she will not be persuaded that her own husband is above her, or has any authority over her.

First, therefore, concerning the general, I will lay down #footnote[Proofs that the husband is above the wife] some clear and undeniable proofs to show that a husband is his wife’s superior and has authority over her. The proofs are these.

1\. God, by whom “the authorities that exist have been appointed,”#footnote[Rom 13:1.] has power to place his image in whom he will; and whatever superiority and authority God gives to anyone ought to be acknowledged as their due. But God said of the man to the woman, “he will rule over you” (Gen 3:16).

2\. Nature has placed an eminence in the male over the #footnote[#emph[Mari & foeminae a natura tributum est, vt hic praesit, illa obediat, cum mas praestantior sit. Arist. Pol. lib.] 1.] female, so that where they are linked together in one yoke, it is given by nature that he should govern and she obey. The heathen observed this by the light of nature.

3\. The titles and names by which a husband is set forth imply a superiority and authority in him, such as “lord”#footnote[Κύριος.] (1 Pet 3:6), #footnote[בעל] “master” (Esth 1:17), #footnote[אלוף] “guide” (Prov 2:17), #footnote[κεφαλή.] “head” (1 Cor 11:3), #footnote[εἰκὼν καὶ δόξα Θεοῦ.] “the image and glory of God” (1 Cor 11:7).

4\. The persons whom the husband by virtue of his place, and the wife by virtue of hers, represent prove as much most clearly. For a #emph[husband] represents #emph[Christ,] and a #emph[wife] the #emph[church] (Eph 5:23).

5\. The circumstances the Holy Spirit notes at the woman’s creation imply no less: that she was created #emph[after man, for man’s good, and out of man’s side] (Gen 2:18, etc.).

6\. The very #footnote[#emph[Si capilli pro velamine dati sunt, qua gratia aliud addendum est velamen? Vt non tantum natura, sed etiam voluntate subiectam se esse confiteatur. Chrys. in] 1 #emph[Cor. hom.] 26. \[If hair is given for a covering, why must another covering be added? So that she may confess herself subject not only by nature but also by her will.\]] attire which nature and the custom of all times and places have taught women to put on confirms the same: #emph[long hair, veils,] and other coverings over the head. The apostle himself uses this argument and the one before it for this very purpose, 1 Cor 11:7, #emph[etc.]

The point, then, being so clear, wives ought in conscience to acknowledge as much: namely, that a husband has superiority #footnote[1\. Wives must acknowledge a superiority in husbands.] and authority over a wife. The acknowledgment of this is a main and principal duty, and a ground of all other duties. Until a wife is fully instructed in it and truly persuaded of it, no duty can be performed by her as it ought to be. For subjection has relation to superiority and authority; the very derivation of the word implies as much. How then can subjection be yielded if husbands are not acknowledged as superiors? It may be forced, as one king conquered in battle by another may be compelled to pay homage to the conqueror. But because he still thinks to himself that he is in no way inferior, he will hardly be brought to yield a subject’s duty to him willingly, but will rather wait for a time when he may free himself and take revenge on the conqueror.

=== §. 4. #emph[Of a foolish notion that husband and wife are equal.]

Contrary to the subjection named above is the opinion of many wives, who think themselves in every way as good as their husbands, and in no way inferior to them.

The reason for this seems to be that small inequality #footnote[Of all inferiors, the wife comes nearest to equality.] which there is between the husband and the wife. For of all the degrees in which there is any difference between person and person, there is the least disparity between man and wife. Though the man is like the head, yet the woman is like the heart, which is the most excellent part of the body next to the head, far more excellent than any other member under the head, almost equal to the head in many respects, and as necessary as the head. As evidence that a wife is to man as the heart to the head, she was at her #footnote[Gen 2:21.] first creation taken out of the side of man, where his heart lies. And though the woman at first “came from man,”#footnote[1 Cor 11:12.] created out of his side, yet “man is born of woman.” Ever since the first creation man has been born and brought forth out of the woman’s womb, so that “man is not independent of woman, nor is woman independent of man.” Yes, “the wife does not have power over her own body, but the husband. Likewise the husband does not have power over his own body, but the wife.”#footnote[1 Cor 7:4. #emph[Cùm de pudicitia sermonem habet, magna paritas. Chrys. hom.] 19. #emph[in] 1 Cor 7. \[When he speaks of chastity, there is great equality.\]] They are also #footnote[1 Pet 3:7 συγκληρονόμοι.] “fellow heirs of the grace of life.” Besides, wives are #emph[mothers] of the same children of whom their husbands are fathers (for God said to both, “Be fruitful and multiply”),#footnote[Gen 1:28.] and #emph[mistresses] of the same servants of whom their husbands are masters (for Sarah is called “mistress”).#footnote[Gen 16:4. #emph[Objection. Answer.]] And in many other respects there is a common equity between husbands and wives. From this many wives gather that in all things there ought to be a mutual equality.

But to infer a general rule from some particulars is a very weak argument.

1\. Does it follow that because in many things there is a common equity between judges of assize, justices of the peace and town constables, there is therefore an equality between them in all things?

2\. In many things there is not a common equity: for the husband may command his wife, but she is not to command him.

3\. Even in those things in which there is a common equity, there is not an equality. For the husband always has a superiority, even in all things: if there is any difference even in the instances named above, the husband must have the final say. So in giving the name of Rachel’s youngest child, when the wife wanted one name and the husband another, the name which the husband gave stood (Gen 35:18).

However little disparity there may seem to be, yet since God has so expressly appointed subjection, it ought to be acknowledged. And #footnote[#emph[Possunt coniuges per charitatem seruire inuicem, sed mulierem non permittit Apostolus dominari in virum. Aug. de Gen. ad lit. l.] 11. #emph[c.] 37.] though husband and wife may serve one another mutually through love, yet the apostle does not allow a woman to rule over the man.

=== §. 5. #emph[Of a wife’s acknowledgment of her own husband’s superiority.]#footnote(numbering: (..) => "*")[Treatise 4, §. 9.]

The truth and life of that general acknowledgment of husbands’ #emph[honor] consists in its particular application to their very #emph[own] husbands.

The next duty, therefore, is that wives acknowledge their #footnote[All wives must acknowledge their own husbands worthy of honor.] #emph[own] husbands, those to whom they are joined in marriage by God’s providence, to be worthy of a husband’s honor and to be their superiors. This is what the apostle intends by that word of restriction#footnote[ἰδίοις. Eph 5:22, 24.] (#emph[own]), which he uses very often. So does Saint Peter,#footnote[1 Pet 3:1, 5.] exhorting wives to be in subjection #footnote[1 Cor 7:2.] to their #emph[own] husbands, and restricting to this the commendation of the good wives of old, that they were in subjection to their #emph[own] husbands.

#emph[Objection.] What if a man of lowly place is married to a woman of eminent place, or a servant is married to his mistress, or an aged woman to a youth? Must such a wife acknowledge such a husband as her superior?

#emph[Answer.] Yes, truly. For in giving herself to be his wife, and #footnote[Wives inferior to those over whom they were before marriage.] taking him to be her husband, she raises him above herself and subjects herself to him. It makes no difference what either of them was before marriage. By virtue of the marriage bond the husband is made the head of his wife, though before marriage the husband was a very beggar, of lowly parentage, and the wife very wealthy and of a noble stock; or though he was her apprentice or her slave. This also holds in the case of an aged woman and a youth: for the Scripture has made no exception in any of these cases.

2\. #emph[Objection.] But what if a man of wicked and beastly character, such as a drunkard, a glutton, a profane swaggerer, an impious swearer and blasphemer, is married to a wife who is a sober, religious matron? Must she account him her superior, and worthy of a husband’s honor?

#emph[Answer.] Surely she must. For the evil quality and disposition #footnote[Subjection due to husbands of evil qualities.] of his heart and life does not deprive a man of that social honor which God has given him. Though because of his evil qualities a husband may carry the image of the devil, yet with respect to his place and office he bears the image of God. So do magistrates in the commonwealth, ministers in the church, and parents and masters in the family. Note for our present purpose the exhortation of Saint Peter to Christian wives who had unbelieving husbands: “Be subject to them; let your#super[+] conduct be coupled with fear.”#footnote[1 Pet 3:1, 2.] If unbelievers do not carry the devil’s image, and are not vassals of Satan as long as they are unbelievers, who are? Yet wives must be subject to them, and fear them.

=== §. 6. #emph[Of wives denying honor to their own husbands.]

Contrary to this is a very perverse disposition in some #footnote[A perverse opinion to think other husbands worthy of honor, and not their own.] wives, who think they could subject themselves to any husband better than to their own. Though in general they acknowledge that a husband is his wife’s superior, yet when it comes to applying it to themselves they fail, and cannot be brought to grant that they are their husbands’ inferiors. This is a vice worse than the former. For to acknowledge no husband as superior over his wife, but to think man and wife equal in all things, may proceed from ignorance of mind and error of judgment. But for a wife who knows and acknowledges the general rule, #emph[that a husband is above his wife,] to imagine that she herself is not inferior to her husband arises from monstrous conceit and intolerable arrogance, as if she herself were above her own sex, and more than a woman.

Contrary also is the practice of such #footnote[See Treatise 2, part 1, §. 9.] women as purposely marry men of far lower rank than themselves, for this very end, that they may rule over their own husbands; and of others who, being #footnote[See #emph[Ibid.]] aged, marry youths, if not mere boys, for the same end. It is a mind and practice very unseemly, and flatly thwarting God’s ordinance. But let them think of ruling #footnote[#emph[Indecens est vt mulier magistra viri fiat. Orig. in Isa. hom.] 7. \[It is unseemly for a woman to become the man’s master.\]] as they please; the truth is that they make themselves subjects by both God’s law and man’s, and such wives often feel the heaviest burden of this subjection. Solomon notes this as one of the things under which the earth trembles: “a #footnote[Prov 30:21, 22.] servant who becomes king.” Now when can a servant domineer more than when he has married his mistress? As for aged women who are married to youths, I may say (as was said in another case), “woe to you, O wife, whose husband is a child.”#footnote[Eccl 10:16.] It is unfitting for an aged man to be married to a young maid, but much more unfitting for an aged woman to be married to a youth.

=== §. 7. #emph[Of a wife’s inward fear of her husband.]#footnote(numbering: (..) => "*")[Treatise 4, §. 11.]

So much for a wife’s acknowledgment of her husband’s superiority. Next we are to speak of the corresponding respect which she ought to bear toward him.

A wifely respect for her husband consists in two points:

+ #emph[Reverence.]
+ #emph[Obedience.]

The reverence which she owes him is #footnote[Wifely fear.]

+ Inward.
+ Outward.

Inward reverence is a respect full of awe which a wife has in her heart for her husband, esteeming him worthy of all honor for the sake of his place and office, because he is her husband. Doubtless Sarah had in her heart a reverent regard and honorable esteem for her husband when, #footnote[Gen 18:12.] being alone and thinking of him in her very thought, she gave him this title, “lord.” This inward reverence the Scripture includes under this word #emph[fear,] as where our apostle says, #footnote[Eph 5:33 φοβῆται.] “let the wife see that she fears her husband,” and where #footnote[1 Pet 3:2 ἐν φόβῳ.] Saint Peter exhorts wives to have their conduct “coupled with #emph[fear.]” It is no slavish fear of her husband which ought to possess the heart of a wife, dreading blows, frowns, spiteful words or the like; but such a respect full of awe for him as makes her (to use the apostle’s word) “care … how she can please her husband.”#footnote[1 Cor 7:34.] This wifely #emph[fear] shows itself in two effects. One is #emph[joy,] when she gives her husband contentment and sees that he is pleased with what she does. The other is #emph[grief,] when he is justly offended and grieved, especially with anything that she herself has done.

Unless this inward reverence and due respect for a husband #footnote[True fear a ground of other duties.] is first placed in the heart of a wife, either no outward reverence and obedience will be performed at all, or, if it is performed, it will be very unsound, only for show, hypocritical and deceitful; so that #emph[it is as good not to do it at all as to be never the better for it.] For the outward action and conduct will be shaped according to one’s inward feeling and disposition. Michal#footnote[2 Sam 6:16.] first despised David “in her heart,” and from this it followed that she #footnote[#emph[v.] 20. How fear may be worked in a wife’s heart.] uttered most irreverent and vile speeches about him, even to his face. Therefore, after the judgment of a wife is rightly informed of a husband’s superiority, and her will persuaded to account her own husband her #emph[head] and #emph[guide,] it is very needful that her heart and feelings be seasoned accordingly with the salt of good respect and high esteem, which breeds #emph[fear.] And so that her heart may be seasoned in this way, she ought often and seriously to meditate on his place and office, and on that honor which the Lord by virtue of it has planted in him. And if he has gifts worthy of his place, such as knowledge, wisdom, piety, temperance, love and the like, she ought to take notice of them, and to think him worthy of double honor.

=== §. 8. #emph[Of a wife’s low esteem of her husband.]

Contrary to this inward reverence of the heart is a low and vile esteem which many have of their husbands, thinking no better of them than of other men, no, worse than of others, and despising their husbands in their heart, like Michal, of whom we heard before. As this is in itself a vile vice, so it is also a cause of many other vices, such as presumption, rebellion, #footnote[A low esteem which wives have of their husbands arises] yes, and many times of adultery itself; and it is also a main hindrance to all duty.

It commonly arises either from conceit (by which wives think too highly of their own gifts, thinking them so excellent #footnote[1\. From conceit.] that they need no guide or head, but are rather fit to guide and rule both their husband and the whole household. Of this proud and presumptuous spirit Jezebel seems to have been, who with a bold and shameless face said to Ahab her husband, “Do you not reign over Israel? Get up … I will get you the vineyard of Naboth.”#footnote[1 Kgs 21:7.] So also were all those wives who are noted for drawing their husbands’ hearts away from the Lord, such as the wives of Solomon,#footnote[1 Kgs 11:4.] Jehoram#footnote[2 Kgs 8:18.] and others, who learned it from their great-grandmother Eve#footnote[Gen 3:6.]); or else from some infirmities of mind or body, or of life, which they see in their #footnote[2\. From their husbands’ infirmities.] husbands (which is how it comes about that many husbands who are highly honored and greatly esteemed by others are much despised by their wives, because their wives, always living with them, are privy to such infirmities as are hidden from others); or, worst of all, from unjust surmises #footnote[3\. From unjust suspicions.] and suspicions, suspecting their husbands of many evil things of which they are in no way guilty, and misinterpreting and perverting things well done, as Michal#footnote[2 Sam 6:20.] perverted David’s holy zeal.

To remedy this monstrous vice, wives ought first, with respect #footnote[How wives may remedy a low esteem of their husbands.] to themselves, to purge pride and conceit out of their hearts, thinking humbly and lowly of themselves, even because of their sex and its weakness. And if the Lord has endowed them with any gift above the ordinary sort of women, they ought to note well their own infirmities and set them beside their eminent gifts: so, by looking at their black feet, their proud peacock feathers may be brought down. Yes, also, when they see any infirmities in their husbands, they ought to turn their eyes back on their own infirmities, which perhaps are just as many and as grievous, if not more in number and more heinous in their nature and kind. At least let them consider that they are subject to the same, if God leaves them to the sway of their own corruption.

Secondly, wives ought, with respect to their husbands, to suspect no evil of which they have no sure proof and evidence, but rather take everything in the better sense, and follow the rule of love, which “bears all things, believes all things, hopes all things, endures all things.”#footnote[1 Cor 13:7.] If they note any defects of nature and deformity of body, or any gross and notorious vices in their husband, then they ought to turn their eyes and thoughts from his person to his place, and from his vicious qualities to his honorable office (which is to be a husband). This will lessen that vile esteem which might otherwise be occasioned by the means named above.

=== §. 9. #emph[Of wifely sobriety.]#footnote(numbering: (..) => "*")[Treatise 4, §. 4.]

A wife’s outward reverence toward her husband is a showing of her inward due respect for him. Now, seeing #footnote[A wife’s outward reverence.] the intent of the heart and the inward disposition cannot be discerned by man in themselves, so that the husband may know his wife’s good feeling toward him, it is needful that she show it by her outward reverence.

A wife’s outward reverence consists in her reverent

- #emph[Bearing.]
- #emph[Speech.]

As for the first, that a reverent bearing and conduct of herself toward #footnote[A wife’s reverence in her bearing.] her husband, and in her husband’s presence, becomes a wife was implied of old by the #emph[veil] which the woman used to put on when she was brought to her husband, as is noted in the #footnote[Gen 24:65.] example of Rebekah. The apostle alludes to this in these words: “a woman ought to have a sign of authority on her head.”#footnote[1 Cor 11:10.] As that covering on the woman’s head implied #emph[subjection] in general, so in particular it implied this kind of subjection, namely #emph[a reverent conduct] and bearing. But this duty is set down most expressly by #footnote[1 Pet 3:1 ἀναστροφὴν ἁγνὴν ἐν φόβῳ.] Saint Peter, who exhorts wives to order their #emph[conduct] before their husbands so that it is pure, #emph[with reverence.]

This reverent conduct consists in a wifely #emph[sobriety, mildness, courtesy,] and #emph[modesty.]

By sobriety I mean such a seemly, serious and gracious bearing as gives evidence to the husband that his wife respects his place and the authority which God has given him. Sobriety in general is required of all women because of their sex, and surely it well becomes them all; but it becomes wives much more, and most of all in their husband’s presence. #footnote[1 Tim 3:11.] The apostle requires it in particular of #emph[deacons’ wives,] yet not as belonging only to them, but as belonging to them in a further respect, not only as wives, but as the wives of deacons.

Contrary to this sobriety are flightiness and wantonness. These vices in a wife, especially before her husband, show little respect for him, if not a plain contempt of him.

#emph[Objection.] In this way all delightful familiarity between husband and wife will be taken away.

#emph[Answer.] Though the sobriety named above is opposed to flightiness #footnote[Difference between wantonness and familiarity.] and wantonness, yet it is not opposed to marital familiarity. This is permitted to man and wife so far that if any other man and woman behaved toward each other as a husband and wife lawfully may, it might justly be counted flightiness and sin. Take the example of Isaac and Rebekah,#footnote[Gen 26:8, 9.] who so caressed each other that Abimelech, knowing them to be people who feared God, gathered from that #emph[caressing] that they were man and wife: for he thought that otherwise they would not have been so familiar together.

This#footnote(numbering: (..) => "*")[See Treatise 4, §. 42.] familiarity proves both #emph[liking] and #emph[love,] and shows that the man and wife delight in one another’s person. But the flightiness here condemned in a wife is not so much a mutual familiarity with her husband, with his good liking, as a wanton dallying with others, to his grief and disgrace.

=== §. 10. #emph[Of wifely mildness.]#footnote(numbering: (..) => "*")[Treatise 4, §. 13, 22.]

Mildness in a wife also has to do with the ordering of her expression, bearing and whole conduct before her husband, by which she shows a pleasingness toward him, and a contentment and willingness to be under him and ruled by him. This is excellently set forth in the spouse of Christ, whose eyes are said to be “like doves,” her lips to “drip sweetness like the honeycomb,” and #footnote[Song 4. #emph[&] 6. #emph[&] 7.] she herself every way “pleasant.” From this it is noted that she appeared to her husband “like the dawn,” and that his heart was wounded by her. Surely the clear sky is not more pleasant in time of harvest than a mild and amiable expression and conduct of a wife in her husband’s presence. #footnote[Is cui leonum cura commissa est, blanda manu, mollique verborum sono permulsos domat. Greg. Naz. ad Olymp.] And though her husband should be of a harsh and cruel disposition, yet by this means he might be made meek and gentle. For the keepers of lions are said to bring them to some tameness by handling them gently and speaking to them kindly.

Contrary to this mildness are a frowning brow, a scowling eye, a sullen look, a pouting lip, a puffed-up face, a mocking mouth, a scornful toss of the arms and hands, a disdainful turning of the body this way and that, and a fretful flinging out of her husband’s presence. All these and other like contemptuous gestures are like thick clouds spreading over the sky on a summer’s day, which make it very cheerless. They often stir up much anger in the man, and bring much harm on the wife herself.

=== §. 11. #emph[Of wifely courtesy and gestures of respect.]#footnote(numbering: (..) => "*")[Treatise 4, §. 15.]

Courtesy is that virtue by which a wife takes occasion to testify her acknowledgment of her husband’s superiority by some outward gesture of respect toward him. Rebekah, as soon as she saw Isaac, whom she had taken for her husband, got down from her camel and came to him on foot, which was a kind of gesture of respect. This is not to be taken as if no difference were to be made between the bearing of a servant or child and that of a wife, or as if a wife should bow at every word that she speaks to her husband. Though in the kind and extent of many duties the same things are required of wives as of children and servants, because God has made them all inferiors and demanded subjection of all, yet in the manner and measure of many duties there is a great difference. So it is in this one: the gestures of respect of children and servants ought to be more submissive and more frequent. Yet because God has placed authority in the husband over his wife, she is in every way to testify her reverent respect for her husband. Therefore at some times and on some occasions (as when he is going away from her on a journey for a time, or when he returns home again, or when she has a solemn and great request to make to him, or when he offers her an especial and extraordinary favor, or (as I have observed in such wives as know what becomes their place, and are not ashamed to show as much) when she sits #footnote[1\. A wife’s courtesy wins her husband’s good regard for her.] down at table or rises from it) she is to declare her reverence by some gesture of respect. This cannot but work much on the heart of a good and kind husband, and make him respect his wife all the more when he sees this evidence of her respect for him. Yes, it cannot but be a good pattern to children #footnote[2\. It makes her husband honored by others.] and servants, and a motive to stir them up to show every submissive gesture of respect both to her husband and to herself. For it may make them reason with themselves like this: “Shall #footnote[3\. It brings honor to herself.] we scorn or begrudge giving our father or master what our mother or mistress does not begrudge giving her husband? Shall she bow to him, and shall we not much more bow to her?” In this way a wife’s honoring of her husband by showing him gestures of respect makes both him and herself more honored by others.

Of a contrary mind are those who not only omit #footnote[Contrary scorn in wives.] this duty altogether, but also jeer and scoff at the very mention of it, saying, “So wives will be made no better than children or servants.” But though scornful dames deride these outward evidences of their subjection, yet wives who fear the Lord ought not to be hindered by this from doing their duty: for by such evil examples they might be discouraged from every good duty. It is enough that such holy women as trusted in God behaved themselves so. But as for this particular, we know that equals do not scorn, when occasion offers, to perform this kind of courtesy in making gestures of respect to one another: how much less should wives, who are their husbands’ inferiors!

=== §. 12. #emph[Of wifely modesty in clothing.]#footnote(numbering: (..) => "*")[Treatise 4, §. 49, 52. 1 Tim 2:9. 1 Pet 3:3.]

The modesty that belongs to a wife is shown much in her clothing. Saint Paul requires this modesty of all sorts of women in general, but Saint Peter presses it in particular on wives. For as it well becomes all women, so it becomes wives in a distinctive way: namely, in dressing themselves, to have regard to their husband’s place and condition rather than to their own birth and parentage, and much rather than to their own mind and fancy. A wife’s modesty therefore requires that her clothing be neither above her husband’s means in costliness, nor unbecoming his calling in its elaborateness. As a poor man’s wife must not seek costly clothing, so neither must the wives of ministers, grave counselors, wise magistrates, no, nor of conscientious professing Christians, hunt after new fashions, or dress themselves in frivolous and garish clothing. It is a token of great reverence in a wife toward her husband to have an eye to his place and condition in her clothing.

On the contrary, such proud dames as must have their own way in their attire, and think it is no business #footnote[Contrary: wives’ attire not fitting their husbands’ condition or place.] of their husbands to order them in it, and do not care what their husband’s means or his place and calling are, show little respect and reverence to their husbands. Such are those who are not at all moved by their husband’s example: though the man’s clothing is plain and grave, yet the wife’s must be costly and garish. Yes, there are many who stand somewhat more in awe of their husband’s sight, but show him little more respect. They have their silk gowns, beaver hats and other such attire not suited to their place and condition lying in the country, if they are of the city, or in the city, if they are of the country, in a friend’s house where their husbands will #footnote[Quae se pingit comitque secus quàm viro gratum esse nouit, satis declarat sese pluribus velle placere quàm oportet mulierem pudicam, cuius praecipua laus est vni placuisse cui desponsata est. Erasm. Instit. Matr. \[She who paints and adorns herself otherwise than she knows is pleasing to her husband shows plainly enough that she wants to please more people than a chaste woman ought, whose chief praise is to have pleased the one to whom she was betrothed.\]] not know of it; and when their husbands are not with them, they wear them, paint their faces, do up their hair, and follow the fashion in everything. What can those who see this think, but that such a wife’s care is more to please other flighty, vain people than her grave, discreet husband, or that her husband can have no influence at all with her? As this stains her own good name, so it leaves a blot of dishonor even on him. If a wife’s care were to give evidence of the reverence which she bears her husband, his desire and example would in this matter prevail more with her than the fancies of her own heart.

=== §. 13. #emph[Of a wife’s reverent speech to her husband.]#footnote(numbering: (..) => "*")[Treatise 4, §. 24.]

As by bearing, so also by speech must a wife’s reverence be shown: #emph[this] must correspond to #emph[that.] For the feeling of the heart is shown by words as well as by deeds: “out of the overflow of the heart, the mouth speaks.”#footnote[Matt 12:34.] A wife’s reverence is shown by her speech both in her husband’s presence and in his absence. To this end, in his presence her words must be few, reverent and meek. First, few: for the #footnote[A wife’s words in her husband’s presence must be few.] apostle requires #emph[silence]#footnote[1 Tim 2:12.] of wives in their husbands’ presence, and enforces that duty with a strong reason in these words: “I do not permit a woman to usurp authority over a man, but to be in silence.” The way the latter clause is drawn from the former shows that he speaks not only of a woman’s silence in the church, but also of a wife’s silence before her husband. This is further cleared by another like place, where the same apostle commands wives to learn from their husbands at home.#footnote[1 Cor 14:35.] The reason mentioned before for silence implies on the one side a reverent subjection, as on the other side too much speech implies a usurping of authority.

#emph[Objection.] Then it seems a wife must always be mute before her husband.

#emph[Answer.] Not at all. For silence in that place is not opposed to speech, as if she should not speak at all, but to loquacity, to talkativeness, to too much chattering. Her husband’s presence must somewhat restrain her tongue, and so her very silence will testify a reverent respect. Otherwise silence, as it is opposed to speech, would imply haughtiness of spirit and stubbornness of heart, which is an extreme contrary to loquacity. But the middle way between both is for a wife to be sparing in speech, to wait for a fit time and just occasion to speak, and to be willing to listen to the word of knowledge coming out of her husband’s mouth. This shows reverence. Elihu#footnote[Job 32:6.] showed the reverent respect which, as a younger man, he bore toward his elders by holding back from speaking while they had anything to say. How much more ought wives to do so, because both of their sex and of their place?

Contrary is the practice of those who must and will do all the #footnote[Contrary: too much chatter.] talking. If their husbands have begun to speak, their slippery tongues cannot wait and hold back until he has finished. If (as they are very hasty and eager to speak) they do not speak before their husbands, they will surely take the words out of his mouth before he has finished. So they disgrace themselves and dishonor their husbands.

=== §. 14. #emph[Of the titles which wives give their husbands.]#footnote(numbering: (..) => "*")[Treatise 4, §. 24.]

As their words must be few, so those few words must be reverent and meek. Both are also implied under the word named above, #footnote[1 Tim 2:12 ἡσυχία.] #emph[silence,] which in the original also means #footnote[1 Tim 2:12 ἡσυχία.] #emph[quietness.]

#emph[Reverence] has to do with the titles by which a wife names her husband; #emph[meekness] with the manner of framing her speech to him. #footnote[Reverent titles to be given to husbands.]

As for the titles which a wife gives her husband in speaking to him or naming him, they must be such as signify superiority, and so savor of reverence. Such are the titles by which husbands are named in the Scripture; #footnote[See §. 3.] they are titles of honor. Such also are the titles which the church (whom our apostle makes a pattern for wives in all subjection) gives to her spouse Christ Jesus, as may be gathered from the #emph[Song of Songs.] It is likely that Sarah usually gave this title “lord” to her husband. For when she had occasion to think of him, this title “lord”#footnote[Gen 18:12.] was at once in her heart; and it would not have risen up so readily if she had not ordinarily used it. When we have occasion to think of anyone, we name them in our hearts according to the usual titles we give them. Among all the other titles, the name “husband,” as it is the most usual, so it is the fittest and most suitable title. It suggests reverence, and does not smack of fussiness and oddity, as these titles do: “Head,” “Guide,” “master,” “man” and the like. Though these are lawful titles, because the Scripture gives them to husbands and they signify superiority, yet because they are unusual and smack of oddity, they are not so fitting. Common use and practice have made it more fitting to add the husband’s surname to this title “master.”

Saint#footnote[1 Pet 3:6.] Peter proves by this argument that Sarah obeyed Abraham: because she called him “lord.”

Contrary to these are those forms of address which suggest equality or inferiority rather than superiority, such as “brother,” “Cousin,” “Friend,” “man,” etc. If a stranger is present, how can he tell by this form of address that the one you are speaking to is your husband? If he notices any marital familiarity between you and your husband, what else can he judge it to be but flightiness and wantonness? Remember the fearful outcome that nearly followed from such forms of address given by Sarah#footnote[Gen 12:19; 20:2.] and Rebekah#footnote[Gen 26:9.] to their husbands. Not unlike these are such as “Sweet,” “Sweeting,” “Heart,” “Sweet-heart,” “love,” “Joy,” “Dear,” etc.; and such as “Duck,” “Chick,” “Pigsny,” etc.; and husbands’ Christian names, such as “John,” “Thomas,” “William,” “Henry,” etc. If these are shortened (as many shorten them, like this: “Jack,” “Tom,” “Will,” “Hall”), they are much more unseemly: servants are usually called so.

But what may we say of those titles given to a husband by his wife, not only often in anger but usually in ordinary speech, which are not fit to be given to the basest men there are, such as “Grub,” “Rogue” and the like, which I am even ashamed to name, except that the sins of women are to be cast like dirt in their faces, so that they may be the more ashamed?

#emph[Objection.] Many of the titles named above are titles of friendship and familiarity.

#emph[Answer.] Subjection is the mark which wives are directed to aim at in their thoughts, words, deeds and whole conduct toward their husbands. Such tokens of familiarity as are not also tokens of subjection and reverence are unbecoming in a wife, because they swerve from that mark.

=== §. 15. #emph[Of wives’ meekness in their speech.]#footnote(numbering: (..) => "*")[Treatise 4, §. 24, etc.]

#emph[Meekness] in a wife’s manner of framing her speech to her husband also commends her reverent respect for him. This is a special effect of that #emph[meek] and #emph[quiet spirit] which Saint Peter requires of wives, a duty he strongly enforces by this weighty argument: “which is precious in God’s sight.”#footnote[1 Pet 3:4.] Is a wife’s meekness precious in God’s sight, and shall wives not hold it both a binding duty and a becoming ornament and grace to them? As the form of words which a wife uses in asking or answering questions, or in any other kind of conversation which she holds with her husband, so her moderation in persisting, arguing and pressing matters, yes, and the mild composure of her expression in speaking, declare her meekness. If she wants to obtain anything from him, she must ask for it kindly, as the Shunammite did.#footnote[2 Kgs 4:10, 22.] If she would move him to perform a binding duty, she must persuade him mildly. If she would restrain him and keep him from doing what is evil, even that she must do with some meekness, as Pilate’s#footnote[Matt 27:19.] wife did. If she has occasion to tell him of a fault, she ought in this to show humility and reverence by observing a fit season and doing it in a gentle manner, as Abigail did.#footnote[1 Sam 25:31, 37.] As she behaved wisely in this respect with her husband, in observing a fit season, so she did also with David, by hinting at his fault rather than plainly reproving him, when she said, “my lord will have no remorse or guilt of conscience over needless bloodshed.” This meekness also requires silence and patience, even when she is reproved.

Contrary is the waspish and shrewish disposition of many #footnote[Contrary: wives’ shrewish speech to their husbands.] wives toward their husbands, who do not care how hastily and rashly they speak to them, like Rachel;#footnote[Gen 30:1.] nor how angrily and scoldingly, like Jezebel;#footnote[1 Kgs 21:7.] nor how disdainfully and spitefully, like Zipporah;#footnote[Exod 4:25, 26.] nor how scoffingly and jeeringly, like Michal;#footnote[2 Sam 6:20.] nor how reproachfully and disgracefully, like Job’s#footnote[Job 2:9.] wife. If they are reproved by their husbands, their husbands will be reproached by them, and they are ready to answer back, not only word for word, but ten for one. Many wives by their shrewish speech show no more respect to their husbands than to their servants, if as much. The least occasion moves them not only to be inwardly angry and fret against them, but also to show it outwardly by scolding and brawling. The very target on which many wives usually spit out their venomous words is their husband: when they are full of resentment, they must vent it on their husbands, and in this their fault is doubled.

Let wives therefore learn, first, to moderate their anger, and then to keep in their tongues with bit and bridle, but most of all to take heed that their husbands do not taste its bitterness, no, not even if they should be provoked by some oversight of their husbands. It is to be noted how Solomon calls the quarrels which are between man and wife “the contentions of a wife,”#footnote[Prov 19:13.] by which he suggests that she is commonly the cause of them, either by provoking her husband or by not bearing with him.

=== §. 16. #emph[Of a wife’s speech about her husband in his absence.]#footnote[Treatise 2, Part 2, §. 36.]

The reverence which a wife bears toward her husband must further be shown by her speech about him in his absence. So Sarah showed her reverence,#footnote[Gen 18:12.] and so must all who desire to be counted the daughters of Sarah. The church, speaking of her spouse,#footnote[Song 5:10. #emph[etc.]] does so with as great reverence as if she had spoken to him. It was for honor and reverence’s sake that the Virgin Mary called Joseph the #emph[father of Jesus]#footnote[Luke 2:48.] when she spoke of him.

This shows that a wife’s reverent speech in her husband’s presence and to his face is not flattery, to please him and fawn on him, but sincerity, to please God and perform her duty.

Those therefore act contrary to their duty who in their husbands’ presence #footnote[Contrary: wives’ bitter speech behind their husbands’ backs.] can offer the fairest and meekest speech that may be to their husbands’ faces, but #footnote[See more of this in Treatise 2, part 2, §. 36.] behind their backs speak most reproachfully of them.
